    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1946, a guide described La Mirada as a small community of 213 surrounded by orange, lemon, walnut, and olive groves.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Mirada%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city’s name means “the look” in Spanish, coined by Andrew McNally and his son‑in‑law Edwin Neff.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Mirada%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "McNally bought 2,200 acres of Rancho Los Coyotes in 1888 for $200,000 and turned it into an olive‑oil and fruit empire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Mirada%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "McNally’s 700‑acre Windermere Ranch was surrounded by olive, orange, and lemon groves, and its produce shipped nationwide.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Mirada%2C_California"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "La Mirada hosts the La Mirada Theatre for the Performing Arts and the Splash! water park, two of its most celebrated attractions.",
